WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.695
Quantization changes the approximations used during inference,

00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:06.455
so quality loss may be subtle rather than a complete failure.

00:00:06.455 --> 00:00:10.000
A response can remain fluent while becoming less precise, more repetitive,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.895
or more likely to miss a constraint.

00:00:11.895 --> 00:00:14.910
Hallucination is influenced by the base model, prompt, data,

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:19.400
and sampling as well as precision; quantization does not create or remove it in isolation,

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.700
but aggressive compression can reduce reliability.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.500
Reasoning tasks may reveal mistakes in intermediate steps or consistency.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.000
Coding tasks can expose errors through compilation, tests, API use,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.612
or adherence to requirements.

00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:34.310
Instruction following should be evaluated with prompts containing several constraints.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.275
Long-context tests should check whether the model retrieves details from early and late

00:00:38.275 --> 00:00:41.276
portions rather than merely producing plausible prose.

00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:43.280
Quality sensitivity is workload-specific.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.640
A conversational assistant may tolerate a compact quantization that is unacceptable for

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.690
structured extraction,

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.320
technical analysis, or domain terminology.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.820
Different model architectures and quantization methods can react differently at the same

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:55.800
nominal bit level.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.650
A strong smaller model may outperform a larger aggressively compressed model.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:03.970
Evaluate outputs with clear criteria: factual correctness, task completion,

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:07.000
format compliance, consistency, and useful uncertainty.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.880
Use deterministic or controlled settings where possible and repeat prompts because

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.500
sampling introduces variation.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.005
Do not assume that a popular quant level is good enough,

00:01:15.005 --> 00:01:17.729
and do not assume every difference is caused by quantization.

00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:21.300
Compare against a higher-precision reference and the real acceptance threshold for the

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:21.600
task.
